so excited to get the chance to talk to  you about this show i just finished  watching season one the other night  uh and it is a blast i'm already kind of  clamoring for  more because where they leave that with  that cliffhanger like i cannot i can't  wait  i know it's an aggressive cliffhanger  for the end of season one  um i'd love to take it back to  before  the show even started how familiar were  you with the resident evil franchise  before you signed on i had played the  games and watched the movies so i was  familiar  when did you officially learn who you'd  be playing in the show i'm wondering  what the casting process was like did  you audition for anybody else was there  you know a case of maybe being up for a  couple different roles like how did you  finally end up  playing jade um so the i sort of got the  the email straight away through for um  for jade to be honest with you um it was  under the guise of the title venus  flytrap and i was like oh what's this  this is interesting  read the script and then about two pages  in you see the word umbrella and i was  like ah  resident evil now i know what's  happening here um  and i pretty much uh spoke with the  producers  kind of got an idea of what they wanted  to do with the character with the show  with the ark with the the world of  resident evil because as a fan you know  i was so curious to see how they would  evolve um  the history of that that is the  franchise  um and then i essentially  whacked a scene on tape  of how i thought this character would  approach this world  um  and then the rest is history  i don't think it's any exaggeration to  say you kick ass on this show like  majorly and it's so fun to watch um  but after working on say a project like  charlie's angels i'm curious as to  whether the training process was any  different for you this time around did  you have to approach stunts any  differently how intensive was that for  you i'm i'm wondering how that all was  well first of all thank you  um second of all um actually the  interesting thing about this process  like you say it wasn't my first rodeo in  the uh in the stunt sphere  especially with action  i had to unlearn a lot of the stuff that  i've been trained in because  a character such as the one in charlie's  angels she's  a trained  operative um  very choreographed fight style very  regimented but this with jade she is not  a superhero she's not a trained  operative  so we wanted it to feel messy we wanted  it to feel dangerous  um so we had to unlearn a lot of the  the that sort of  regimented fighting style so we did a  lot of jiu jitsu which is a lot of  grappling  um it's a lot of  moment by moment decision making which  is exactly what we needed for the  character  so that you know hopefully when you  watch these sequences you do feel very  much on the edge of your seat  and it does look a little bit more  disheveled than something that is very  polished there are definitely shots in  this for your character specifically  that look like they are pulled right  from the games i'm thinking specifically  there's a shot i won't go into spoilers  but there's a shot from first person  perspective where jade is like stumbling  around disoriented and i was like this  is a video game  that was something that was so important  to us for the producers um to really  make  you know that feeling from resident evil  feel real in this series  and that's something that even if you  notice it even if you don't you still  get that feeling  um i think that's something that you get  definitely if you're a fan of the  franchise you're kind of in a i would  say uniquely shared position on this  show because you're you're part of a  group of actresses who are playing  one of two versions of the same  character because as we find out in the  show  you know it's set between past and  present with a difference of i believe  it's about 14 years between the two  um i'm wondering how collaborative it  was for you to getting to work with you  know the other actresses and like  especially you know tamara playing the  younger version like were there was  there anything you got to watch from her  performance anything that you tried to  kind of take from her to bring into your  own  how collaborative of a process so i mean  this was an example of you know two  people being cast who really had a  strong grasp of what we wanted to do  with the character  um  we  met for the first time on set in south  africa uh so the chemistry that we had  even though we weren't working together  it was more the sort of um  just a s  seamless streamlined one of those  sibilant words  um  take on  on jade wesker  and it was such a pleasure being able to  watch tamara  on her timeline which is a lot cleaner  than mine  um sort of make the decisions that she  made as a younger jade which informed a  lot of the decisions that she made in  2036 a lot of the traumas that happened  to her in the past and how they carry on  into the present  um  even something as small as her  unapologetic  stubbornness and  sort of fire that she has which then  comes out in moments in 2036 but also  seeing the guilt that she carries  through both timelines um i think adds a  very human and um engaging character arc  for her  one of the themes in the show that feels  so important is family and we get to see  that take shape in a lot of different  ways you know in the past there's  definitely we would say more of like a  nuclear family that's established but  then in the in the future  jade also has her own sense of family  that she has established  which  starts i would say is shattered a little  bit  by the sudden reveal that billy is kind  of still around you know someone who she  had believed to have been gone um  how does that kind of inform her arc  that revelation and how it plays out for  the season  jay does her absolute best  in the past to stand by her sister as  much as possible to the point where you  know it gets her into trouble  and she realizes that and she carries  this responsibility in this guilt like  that i've previously mentioned all the  way up until 2036.  um  obviously the traumatic events of the t  virus and umbrella taking over happens  in those 14 years in between  which lands her in 2036 where  we're in this sort of survival mode way  of of human life she's done her best to  create her own family to try and do the  thing do everything in the best way that  she can  but still  this is the interesting part about jade  is that you also you love her and you  kind of dislike her because there's a  lot of things that she does that are  very questionable she's she's out away  from her family for six months  which is you know with her daughter who  is going through her formative years  whereas she's also out there trying to  solve the t virus figure out a cure for  that and  learn more about the zeros to try and  make the world a better place for her  daughter  so it's a very interesting um mix  between  responsibility and guilt but also this  undying  need to try and um  problem solve on her own  uh she has like an innate need to be  independent and to fix things because  she broke them so aggressively in the  past i personally cannot wait to see  what happens i mean i'm crossing my  fingers already for season two because i  feel like like we said before the  cliffhanger on this season  i have to know what happens and i  haven't feeling plenty more same  i know  i'm sure plenty more fans are going to  be feeling the same way i am all out of  time but thank you so much for taking  the time out of your day to talk to me  about this show i can't wait for  everybody else to to see you in it  because you like i said before you  really kick some ass so  thank you so much of course thank you  you
 

